---
import SEOHead from '../components/SEOHead.astro';
const title = "Shikaku Tips & Strategies — Beginner to Advanced | GridPaw";
const description = "Shikaku tips and strategies for every level: start with primes, master orphan-cell elimination, chain forced placements, and avoid the mistakes that stall you.";

const faqs = [
  { q: "What is the best first move in Shikaku?", a: "Place every 1 as a single cell, then look at prime numbers (2, 3, 5, 7) sitting in corners or on edges. Primes can only form straight strips, and edges cut their options in half, so these are the most reliable opening deductions." },
  { q: "How do I get better at Shikaku fast?", a: "Practise one technique at a time. Spend a few boards only looking for orphan cells (empty cells that just one number can reach). Then spend a few boards only counting area. Isolating techniques builds recognition faster than solving casually." },
  { q: "Is there a trick for big numbers like 12 or 16?", a: "List every shape (for 12: 1×12, 2×6, 3×4 and their rotations), then eliminate each one that runs off the grid or contains a second number. Large numbers usually have only one or two survivors because they collide with so many neighbours." },
  { q: "What should I do when I am completely stuck?", a: "Stop looking at the numbers and look at the empty cells instead. Find the empty cell farthest from any unplaced number, or in the tightest corner, and ask which numbers could reach it. Usually only one can, and that gives you a forced placement." },
  { q: "Do I ever need to try a rectangle and see what happens?", a: "Advanced solvers sometimes test a placement mentally to find a contradiction, but this is still logic, not guessing: you follow the consequences until something breaks, then rule that shape out. On GridPaw you can use Undo to test safely, but aim to do it in your head." },
];
